"128_winui_help.mst — Help, the last of the Mac's four views to reach Windows.

 The Mac's is `world/71_cocoahelp.mst`: a topic sidebar, a find field, and a
 document view with real typography. Its ONE structural idea is worth more
 than any of that and is copied exactly: EVERY WORD LIVES IN THIS FILE, as
 Smalltalk methods. v1 read `docs/macvm_help.md` relative to the process's
 working directory and broke under any launcher that was not its own script.
 An environment that cannot explain itself unless you started it from the
 right folder is not documented, it is merely accompanied.

 THE CONTENT IS NOT COPIED, and that is not laziness. Half of the Mac's text
 describes Cocoa — NSWindows, the Tools menu's own conventions, an
 AppleScript surface this port does not have and will not. Prose that told a
 Windows user about `⌘-D` would be worse than no prose at all. What is copied
 is the SHAPE: eleven-ish topics from `Welcome` to `Under the Hood`, each a
 method, each answering plain lines.

 IT IS A TOOL WINDOW, where the Mac also puts it, and its reader is a
 `textGrid` pane — the declarative UI's third pane type, and the first thing
 to use it. That is deliberate: long prose in a stack of STATIC controls
 would be a paragraph per control and a rebuild per scroll, where the cell
 grid is one upload and is what every reading surface in this shell already
 uses.

 ASCII ONLY, asserted per character in the tests. A guest String is UTF-8
 bytes and the grid takes codepoints, so one em-dash arrives as three cells
 of rubbish — the sixth sighting of that trap in this port, and the first
 where the whole file is prose."

Object subclass: WinHelp [
    <classVars: Topic Scroll>

    "── the topics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Title and selector. `Array with:` reaches five and these are two, but
     the Mac's own comment about that family topping out is worth keeping in
     mind for anyone who extends this table."

    WinHelp class >> topics ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | t |
        t := OrderedCollection new.
        t add: { 'Welcome'.      #welcomeText }.
        t add: { 'The Shell'.    #shellText }.
        t add: { 'Smalltalk'.    #languageText }.
        t add: { 'Tools'.        #toolsText }.
        t add: { 'Games'.        #gamesText }.
        t add: { 'Under the Hood'. #internalsText }.
        ^t
    ]

    WinHelp class >> topic ^ <Integer> [ ^Topic isNil ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ Topic ] ]
    WinHelp class >> scroll ^ <Integer> [ ^Scroll isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ Scroll ] ]
    WinHelp class >> topicTitle ^ <String> [ ^(self topics at: self topic) at: 1 ]

    WinHelp class >> lines ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "The current topic's text, split into lines. Answered as a collection
         rather than a String because everything downstream - the reader, the
         scroll, the tests - counts in lines."
        ^self linesOf: (self perform: ((self topics at: self topic) at: 2))
    ]

    WinHelp class >> linesOf: aString <String> ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | out run |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        run := ''.
        aString do: [ :ch |
            ch asInteger = 10
                ifTrue: [ out add: run. run := '' ]
                ifFalse: [ run := run , (String with: ch) ] ].
        out add: run.
        ^out
    ]

    "── the reader's shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> cols ^ <Integer> [ ^76 ]
    WinHelp class >> rows ^ <Integer> [ ^26 ]

    "The lines actually on screen, and the scroll CLAMPED - a topic shorter
     than the window must not be scrollable into empty space, and one longer
     must not be scrollable past its last line."
    WinHelp class >> visibleLines ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | all out top |
        all := self lines.
        top := self clampedScroll.
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        top + 1 to: ((top + self rows) min: all size) do: [ :i |
            out add: (all at: i) ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinHelp class >> clampedScroll ^ <Integer> [
        ^(self scroll max: 0) min: ((self lines size - self rows) max: 0)
    ]

    "── colour by line KIND, which is all the markup this needs ─────────
     A heading is a line the author underlined or prefixed; a bullet starts
     with a dash; everything else is prose. Deciding that HERE, purely, is
     what lets the tests assert the rendering without a window."

    WinHelp class >> colourOf: aLine <String> ^ <Integer> [
        aLine isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^16r000000 ].
        (aLine startsWith: '# ') ifTrue: [ ^16r0000C0 ].
        (aLine startsWith: '- ') ifTrue: [ ^16r006000 ].
        (aLine startsWith: '  ') ifTrue: [ ^16r606060 ].
        ^16r000000
    ]

    "── the spec ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> spec ^ <WinNode> [
        | buttons |
        buttons := OrderedCollection new.
        1 to: self topics size do: [ :i |
            buttons add: (WinSpec
                button: ((i = self topic ifTrue: [ '* ' ] ifFalse: [ '' ])
                            , ((self topics at: i) at: 1))
                event: ('topic' , i printString) asSymbol
                id: ('topic' , i printString) asSymbol) ].
        ^WinSpec window: 'Help' id: #helpWin body: (WinSpec stack: {
            WinSpec heading: self topicTitle id: #title.
            WinSpec grid: buttons columns: 3 id: #topicGrid.
            WinSpec textGrid: #reader columns: self cols rows: self rows.
            WinSpec row: {
                WinSpec button: 'Page up' event: #pageUp id: #bUp.
                WinSpec button: 'Page down' event: #pageDown id: #bDown.
                WinSpec text: 'line ' , (self clampedScroll + 1) printString
                        , ' of ' , self lines size printString id: #where
            } id: #nav
        } id: #root)
    ]

    "── events ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> handle: event <Symbol> value: value <Object> [
        event == #pageUp ifTrue: [
            Scroll := self clampedScroll - (self rows - 2).
            ^self invalidate ].
        event == #pageDown ifTrue: [
            Scroll := self clampedScroll + (self rows - 2).
            ^self invalidate ].
        1 to: self topics size do: [ :i |
            event == ('topic' , i printString) asSymbol ifTrue: [
                Topic := i.
                Scroll := 0.
                ^self invalidate ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinHelp class >> invalidate [
        (WinToolWindow named: #help) ifNotNil: [ :t | t invalidatePane: #reader ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── the reader ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> paintReaderOn: hwnd <Integer> [
        "The cell grid, filled from `visibleLines` and nothing else. Whole
         every time and unapologetically: a text pane's `damage` is almost
         always `all of it` - a scroll moves every row - and 76x26 cells is
         two thousand stores, which is a thousandth of what the sprite grid
         was doing before its damage rule."
        | base row col |
        hwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (WinRender cellWidthFor: hwnd) = 0 ifTrue: [
            ([ WinRender attach: hwnd points: 9 dpi: WinShell editorMetricsDpi ]
                on: Error do: [ :e | false ]) ifFalse: [ ^self ] ].
        base := WinRender gridFor: hwnd cols: self cols rows: self rows.
        base = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        WinRender clear: hwnd to: 16r000000 on: 16rFFFFFF.
        row := 0.
        self visibleLines do: [ :line |
            row < self rows ifTrue: [
                col := 0.
                line do: [ :chr |
                    col < self cols ifTrue: [
                        WinRender at: base col: col row: row cols: self cols
                            put: (chr asInteger < 32 ifTrue: [ 32 ] ifFalse: [ chr asInteger ])
                            fg: (self colourOf: line)
                            bg: 16rFFFFFF ].
                    col := col + 1 ].
                row := row + 1 ] ].
        WinRender caretFor: hwnd col: 0 row: 0 visible: false.
        WinRender present: hwnd.
        ^self
    ]

    "── registration ────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> install [
        WinToolWindow
            register: #help title: 'Help' width: 720 height: 760
            render: [ WinHelp spec ]
            handler: [ :event :value |
                WinHelp handle: event value: value.
                (WinToolWindow named: #help) rerender ]
            paint: [ :tool :paneId :region |
                paneId = 'reader' ifTrue: [
                    WinHelp paintReaderOn: (tool paneHwnd: #reader) ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── the content, and every word of it is here ───────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> nl ^ <String> [ ^String with: (Character value: 10) ]

    WinHelp class >> welcomeText ^ <String> [
        ^'# MACVM on Windows/ARM64', self nl,
'', self nl,
'This is a Smalltalk environment whose user interface is written in', self nl,
'Smalltalk. The window you are reading this in was built from a', self nl,
'description, by code you can open and edit, in the image that is', self nl,
'running it.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Where to start', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Workspace: type an expression, press Ctrl-D to run it.', self nl,
'- Browser: read and change any class in the image.', self nl,
'- Demos menu: nine upstream games, running unedited.', self nl,
'- Tools menu: the Sprite Editor and the Sound Editor.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The one idea worth having first', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Nothing here is a special case. The shell is objects, the games are', self nl,
'objects, this help text is a method on a class called WinHelp, and', self nl,
'all of it is reachable from the Browser while it runs.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  Try it: open the Browser, find WinHelp, and read welcomeText.', self nl,
'  You are looking at its output.'
    ]

    WinHelp class >> shellText ^ <String> [
        ^'# The shell', self nl,
'', self nl,
'The environment is one native window with a bar of views along the', self nl,
'top, and tool windows summoned from the Tools menu.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The views', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Workspace   an expression scratchpad. Ctrl-D runs, Ctrl-P prints.', self nl,
'- Browser     classes, methods, and Accept to compile.', self nl,
'- Outliner    the image as a live tree.', self nl,
'- Find        senders, implementors, and text across the world.', self nl,
'- Editor      a file-scale editor over the same code.', self nl,
'- Debugger    a halted process, its frames, and F5/F10/F11.', self nl,
'- Canvas      pixels: the demos draw here.', self nl,
'- Tests       SUnit over the whole world corpus.', self nl,
'- Transcript  what the system has been saying.', self nl,
'- Metrics     memory, JIT, code, allocation, GC.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Two VMs', self nl,
'', self nl,
'There are two Smalltalk VMs in this process. The UI VM owns the', self nl,
'window and the message loop; the PRIMARY runs your code. That is', self nl,
'why a long computation does not freeze the window, and why a demo', self nl,
'keeps drawing while you type in the Workspace.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  A doit you run in the Workspace is sent to the primary and its', self nl,
'  answer comes back. The window keeps pumping the whole time.'
    ]

    WinHelp class >> languageText ^ <String> [
        ^'# Smalltalk in one screen', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Everything is an object. You do things by SENDING MESSAGES.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  3 + 4                    a binary message, + , to 3', self nl,
'  Transcript showCr: ''hi''  a keyword message, showCr:', self nl,
'  42 printString           a unary message', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The three kinds, in precedence order', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Unary    binds tightest:   2 factorial + 1  is 3', self nl,
'- Binary   next:             2 + 3 * 4        is 20, not 14', self nl,
'- Keyword  loosest:          d at: 1 put: 2', self nl,
'', self nl,
'There is no operator precedence beyond that. Use parentheses.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Blocks', self nl,
'', self nl,
'A block is code in brackets, an object you can pass and call.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  [ :x | x * x ] value: 5          answers 25', self nl,
'  1 to: 5 do: [ :i | ... ]         a loop is a message', self nl,
'  x > 0 ifTrue: [ ... ]            so is a conditional', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Defining a class', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  Object subclass: Point2 [', self nl,
'      | x y |', self nl,
'      Point2 class >> x: ax y: ay [ ^self new setX: ax y: ay ]', self nl,
'      setX: ax y: ay [ x := ax. y := ay. ^self ]', self nl,
'      + other [ ^Point2 x: x + other x y: y + other y ]', self nl,
'  ]'
    ]

    WinHelp class >> toolsText ^ <String> [
        ^'# Tools', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Tool windows are separate top-level windows, summoned from the', self nl,
'Tools menu and hidden rather than destroyed when closed, so their', self nl,
'state survives and reopening is instant.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Sprite Editor', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Draws the 16-colour sprites the games use.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Click the grid to paint, click a swatch to choose a colour.', self nl,
'- pencil paints one cell, fill floods, pick takes a colour.', self nl,
'- Frames: Add, Dup, Del, and First/Prev/Next to move between them.', self nl,
'- Save writes a sheet CLASS into the image. It is only source.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  A saved sheet is used from a game like this:', self nl,
'    | s | s := MySheet installOn: pane. s moveTo: 40 y: 60.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Sound Editor', self nl,
'', self nl,
'An instrument panel over the same synthesizer the games use.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Play auditions the recipe. Preset steps the twelve built-ins.', self nl,
'- Random rolls a new one; Mutate nudges what you have.', self nl,
'- The picture is the envelope: attack, decay, sustain, release,', self nl,
'  with the frequency sweep drawn under it in amber.', self nl,
'- Save writes a sheet class, as the Sprite Editor does.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# How a tool is written', self nl,
'', self nl,
'A tool is three things: a title, a block that answers a SPEC, and', self nl,
'a block that handles an EVENT. No window code, no control code, no', self nl,
'layout code. See WinDemoTool for the smallest complete example.'
    ]

    WinHelp class >> gamesText ^ <String> [
        ^'# Games', self nl,
'', self nl,
'The Demos menu runs upstream''s games unedited. That is the point:', self nl,
'the same Smalltalk runs on mac, windows, arm and x64, and a game', self nl,
'we had to edit to load would disprove it.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- Life, FreeCell, Minesweeper, Julia, Attractor, Plasma,', self nl,
'  Text Pages, Copper, Breakout, Galaxigans.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Escape stops a running demo. So does Demos > Stop Demo.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The pane is a fantasy console', self nl,
'', self nl,
'GamePane is a hardware SPEC, not a drawing library. A game writes', self nl,
'to emulated hardware the way a cartridge writes to a video chip:', self nl,
'', self nl,
'- an indexed pixel plane, one byte per pixel', self nl,
'- a palette, where changing one entry recolours the whole screen', self nl,
'- a text plane of character cells', self nl,
'- hardware sprites with their own 16 colours', self nl,
'- a background shader, which is the platform''s own', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# Shaders are the hardware emulation', self nl,
'', self nl,
'A game ships one shader source, in Metal. This port translates it', self nl,
'to HLSL and compiles it at runtime. Where a machine has no shader,', self nl,
'the software path draws the same scene - the demo degrades to the', self nl,
'compiler, never to a black screen.'
    ]

    WinHelp class >> internalsText ^ <String> [
        ^'# Under the hood', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The door', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Windows messages reach Smalltalk through one function with a', self nl,
'CLOSED list of messages it will route. Everything else goes', self nl,
'straight to DefWindowProc. A handler RECORDS and returns; the work', self nl,
'happens later, in a drain pass, where the VM is provably not', self nl,
'inside a callback.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The declarative UI', self nl,
'', self nl,
'A tool window''s contents are DATA. The tool answers a tree of', self nl,
'nodes; the realizer builds controls from it. When state changes', self nl,
'the tool answers a NEW tree, and a differ works out the smallest', self nl,
'change: one caption, not one window.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'  Node identity is (type, id). Same identity, changed props means', self nl,
'  patch in place. Different identity means rebuild that subtree.', self nl,
'  When the differ cannot express a change it says so, and the', self nl,
'  whole window is republished. Declining is the feature.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Pixels are outside the spec, so panes have their own channel: a', self nl,
'handler says what it dirtied, and only that is repainted.', self nl,
'', self nl,
'# The JIT', self nl,
'', self nl,
'Methods are interpreted until they are hot, then compiled. Loops', self nl,
'tier up on their back edges and switch to compiled code while', self nl,
'still running - on-stack replacement. Metrics shows the counts.'
    ]

    "── the scripted surface ────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinHelp class >> helpTopic ^ <String> [ ^self topicTitle ]
    WinHelp class >> helpLineCount ^ <Integer> [ ^self lines size ]
    WinHelp class >> helpShow: i <Integer> [ ^self handle: ('topic' , i printString) asSymbol value: nil ]
]

WinHelp install.
